  "query_preview": "very moist sub-tropical airmass. By Sunday the mid/upper\nhighs are both drifting east over east Texas/lower Mississippi\nValley. Dewpoints bump back up mostly into the 50s, while PWs\nincrease back to around .9 inches north to 1.2 inches south. Thus\nwe should see a tick up in thunderstorms, with a more typical\npattern of scattered early afternoon mountain storms, and isolated \nlate afternoon/evening storms over the lowlands. \n\nMonday and Tuesday...the upper high moves over the southern\nAppalachians, continuing the deep southerly flow over the CWA. PWs\nincrease to 1.2 to 1.4 inches and dewpoints remain well into the\n50s. Thunderstorm coverage will increase all areas, with lower\nrisk of flash flooding. \n\nWednesday into Saturday...the upper high begins retracing it's \npath back west, over the lower Mississippi Valley Thursday and \nover southern Texas Friday. PWs increase to 1.3 to 1.5 inches \nthrough Saturday. Dewpoints remain in the 50s to lower 60s. Expect\never increasing coverage of thunderstorms, with flash flood risk \nrising to moderate levels. The models, especially the GFS, \ncontinue to show weak disturbances moving up in the southerly \nflow. By Thursday night one of these disturbances opens up into a \nbroad, but weak trough across southern Arizona and New Mexico. By \nFriday this trough phases in with a Polar Jet trough sweeping \nacross the central Rockies. Thus plenty of moisture will remain in\na stretched out deformation zone extending east-west across\ncentral New Mexico and southern Arizona. PWs continue as high as\n1.5 inches both Friday/Saturday, so good storm coverage with a\nflash flood risk should continue. \n\nSunday and beyond...the Polar Jet is able to drop southward over \nNew Mexico, pushing the deformation zone southward and ushering\ndrier air into the area. Thus storm coverage should dwindle to\nisolated with flood risk mostly done. Looking at the GFS-360 and \nthe CFS long range, this may usher in the end of the monsoon\nseason by the early 20s of September.",
